course: dictionary
lesson: 6736
title: The blind cave salamander
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: You can learn a lot about an unknown animal by reading the clues around its name. «The olm is a blind, pink cave salamander from Slovenia and Croatia that lives in water.» Blind, cave and water tell you where it lives and what it can do.
explain: Blind means it cannot see. So an olm cannot use its eyes to find food in the dark. Ask yourself: what else could help it find its way? The lessons say it uses smell, hearing and a special electricity sense.
explain: «The olm lives in dark caves in Slovenia and nearby; it has no colour, and its eyes are hidden under skin.» Each sentence adds one more clue. Put the clues together and you get a clear picture of the animal.
fact: An olm is a salamander, which is a type of amphibian.
fact: Olms live in dark underground cave waters in southern Europe, especially in Slovenia and Croatia.
fact: Olms are pinkish-white, with tiny legs and red feathery gills.
fact: An olm can live for many decades without much food.
fact: Scientists use the olm as an example of adaptation to darkness: it has no working eyes and finds food using smell, hearing and electricity sense.
exercise: Where does the olm live? | In hot deserts in Africa | In busy city ponds in Asia | On tall mountains in the Arctic | *In dark cave waters in southern Europe
exercise: The olm is blind. How does it find food? | *Using smell, hearing and electricity sense | By reading maps on the water | By flying above the caves | Using its big bright eyes
exercise: Find and copy one word from «The olm is a blind, pink cave salamander from Slovenia and Croatia» that means cannot see. | pink | salamander | *blind | cave
test: What colour are olms? | *Pinkish-white | Black with yellow stripes | Orange with spots | Bright green
test: Scientists use the olm as an example of what? | Hibernating in winter | Flying long distances | *Adaptation to darkness | Migrating across oceans
exam: The olm can live for about how many years? | About two weeks | *About a hundred years | About five minutes | About one day
